   "explain": "A. Correct because the specific capital allocation line that uses the market portfolio as the optimal risky portfolio is known as the capital market line (CML). Further, theoretically, the market includes all risky assets. Because the CML assumes homogeneous expectations, only one optimal (risky) portfolio exists.\nB. Incorrect because an investor's optimal portfolio (the combination of the risk-free asset and the market portfolio that maximizes an individual investor's utility) could be any point on the CML. The location of an optimal investor portfolio (not the optimal risky portfolio) depends on the investor's risk preferences. A highly risk-averse investor may invest a large proportion, even 100 percent, of his/her assets in the risk-free asset. A less risk-averse investor, however, may invest a large portion of his/her wealth in the optimal risky asset.\nC. Incorrect because the market portfolio is the optimal risky portfolio rather than a combination of the risk-free asset and a portfolio of risky assets. All combinations of the risk-free asset and a risky asset lie on the capital allocation line. Superimposing an investor's indifference curves on the capital allocation line will lead to the optimal investor portfolio. An investor's optimal portfolio could be any point on the CML."
